Title: How do I merge two dictionaries in a single expression in Python?.
Body: <p>I want to merge two dictionaries into a new dictionary.</p>
<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}
y = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}
z = merge(x, y)

&gt;&gt;&gt; z
{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'c': 4}
</code></pre>
<p>Whenever a key <code>k</code> is present in both dictionaries, only the value <code>y[k]</code> should be kept.</p>

Answers:
```python
def merge(x, y):
    result = {}
    for k, v in x.items():
        if k in y:
            result[k] = y[k]
        else:
            result[k] = v
    return result

x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}
y = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}
z = merge(x, y)

print(z)
```

This code defines a function `merge` that takes two dictionaries `x` and `y` as input and returns a new dictionary `z` that is the result of merging `x` and `y`. The function iterates over the items in `x` and checks if the corresponding key is present in `y`. If it is, the value from `y` is kept; otherwise, the value from `x` is kept. This approach ensures that keys present in both dictionaries are preserved.

