Title: How do I check if an array includes a value in JavaScript?.
Body: <p>What is the most concise and efficient way to find out if a JavaScript array contains a value?</p>
<p>This is the only way I know to do it:</p>
<pre><code>function contains(a, obj) {
    for (var i = 0; i &lt; a.length; i++) {
        if (a[i] === obj) {
            return true;
        }
    }
    return false;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Is there a better and more concise way to accomplish this?</p>
<p>This is very closely related to Stack Overflow question <em><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/143847/best-way-to-find-an-item-in-a-javascript-array">Best way to find an item in a JavaScript Array?</a></em> which addresses finding objects in an array using <code>indexOf</code>.</p>

Title: What does O(log n) mean exactly?.
Body: <p>I am learning about Big O Notation running times and amortized times.  I understand the notion of <em>O(n)</em> linear time, meaning that the size of the input affects the growth of the algorithm proportionally...and the same goes for, for example, quadratic time <em>O(n<sup>2</sup>)</em> etc..even algorithms, such as permutation generators, with <em>O(n!)</em> times, that grow by factorials.</p>

<p>For example, the following function is <em>O(n)</em> because the algorithm grows in proportion to its input <em>n</em>:</p>

<pre><code>f(int n) {
  int i;
  for (i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i)
    printf("%d", i);
}
</code></pre>

<p>Similarly, if there was a nested loop, the time would be O(n<sup>2</sup>).</p>

<p>But what exactly is <em>O(log n)</em>?  For example, what does it mean to say that the height of a complete binary tree is <em>O(log n)</em>?</p>

<p>I do know (maybe not in great detail) what Logarithm is, in the sense that:  log<sub>10</sub> 100 = 2, but I cannot understand how to identify a function with a logarithmic time.</p>

Answers:
import math

def binary_search(arr, target):
    left, right = 0, len(arr) - 1
    while left <= right:
        mid = (left + right) // 2
        if arr[mid] == target:
            return mid
        elif arr[mid] < target:
            left = mid + 1
        else:
            right = mid - 1
    return -1

def find_height(root):
    if root is None:
        return 0
    else:
        return 1 + max(find_height(root.left), find_height(root.right))

def optimal_2048_algorithm():
    # Initialize the game state
    game_state = {
        '2': 0,
        '4': 0,
        'X': 0
    }

    # Initialize the move counters
    merge_for_2 = 0
    merge_for_4 = 0

    # Main game loop
    while True:
        # Try to merge tiles with values 2 and 4
        if game_state['2'] < game_state['4']:
            merge_for_2 += game_state['2']
            game_state['2'] = 0
            game_state['4'] += 1
        else:
            merge_for_4 += game_state['4']
            game_state['4'] = 0
            game_state['2'] += 1

        # Check if the game is over
        if game_state['2'] == 0 or game_state['4'] == 0:
            break

        # Check for optimal moves
        for i in range(4):
            for j in range(4):
                if game_state[(i * 4) + j] == '2' or game_state[(i * 4) + j] == '4':
                    new_game_state = game_state.copy()
                    new_game_state[(i * 4) + j] = 0
                    if (i + 1) * 4 + j < 4 and new_game_state[(i + 1) * 4 + j] == 0:
                        new_game_state[(i + 1) * 4 + j] = game_state[(i * 4) + j] * 2
                        if new_game_state[(i + 1) * 4 + j] == 0:
                            new_game_state[(i + 1) * 4 + j] = 0
                    elif (i - 1) * 4 + j < 4 and new_game_state[(i - 1) * 4 + j] == 0:
                        new_game_state[(i - 1) * 4 + j] = game_state[(i * 4) + j] * 2
                        if new_game_state[(i - 1) * 4 + j] == 0:
                            new_game_state[(i - 1) * 4 + j] = 0
                    elif i * 4 + j < 4 and new_game_state[i * 4 + j] == 0:
                        new_game_state[i * 4 + j] = game_state[(i * 4) + j] * 2
                    if new_game_state[(i * 4) + j] == 2048:
                        game_state[(i * 4) + j] = 0
                        game_state['2'] = 0
                        game_state['4'] = 0
                        break

        # Update the game state
        game_state = new_game_state

    # Return the total score
    return game_state['2'] + game_state['4']

