To restrict the upload of only image files through the `<input type="file">` tag, you can use the `accept` attribute in HTML. This attribute allows you to specify the allowed file types. You can also use the `type` attribute in JavaScript to restrict the file types that can be uploaded. Here's an example:

HTML:
```html
<input type="file" accept="image/*" multiple />
```

JavaScript:
```javascript
const fileInput = document.getElementById('fileInput');

fileInput.addEventListener('change', function(event) {
  const files = event.target.files;
  const imageExtensions = ['.jpg', '.gif', '.bmp', '.tiff', '.png']; // add more extensions as needed
  const validExtensions = imageExtensions.filter(extension => {
    const regex = new RegExp(extension, 'i');
    return files.some(file => regex.test(file.name));
  });

  if (validExtensions.length > 0) {
    event.target.value = validExtensions.join(', ');
  }
});
```

In this example, the `accept` attribute is set to `image/*`, which allows only image files to be uploaded. The `multiple` attribute is also set to `true` to allow multiple file uploads. The JavaScript code checks if any valid image extensions are present in the uploaded files and sets the `value` attribute of the `<input>` element to the valid extensions.

Questions:
Title: Is it possible to set a src attribute of an img tag in CSS?.
Body: <p>Is it possible to set the <code>src</code> attribute value in CSS? <br>
In most cases, we use it like this:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;img src=&quot;pathTo/myImage.jpg&quot; /&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>and I want it to be something like this</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;img class=&quot;myClass&quot; /&gt;
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>.myClass {
    some-src-property: url(&quot;pathTo/myImage.jpg&quot;);
}
</code></pre>
<p>I want to know if there is a way doing it <strong>without</strong> using the <code>background</code> or <code>background-image</code> properties in CSS.</p>

Answers:
You can set a src attribute of an img tag in CSS by using the @import rule or by adding the src attribute directly to the img tag's style attribute.

Here is an example of how you can achieve this:

```css
.my-class {
  background: url('pathTo/myImage.jpg');
  background-size: cover;
  background-position: center;
}
```

Or, you can use the @import rule to import an image and set its src attribute:

```css
@import url('pathTo/myImage.jpg');

.my-class {
  background: url(@import url('pathTo/myImage.jpg'));
  background-size: cover;
  background-position: center;
}
```

You can also use the src attribute directly on the img tag, but this is not recommended as it can lead to inconsistencies and make the code harder to maintain.

HTML
```
<style>
  body {
    background-image: url('image.png');
  }
</style>
```
CSS
```
body {
  background-image: linear-gradient(to bottom, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0), rgba(255, 255, 255, 0));
}
```

