To clear the value of an input file control using jQuery, you can use the `attr()` method with the `value` attribute and set it to an empty string. However, this approach only works if the file input control is a direct child of the element with the id `control`. 

If the file input control is a descendant of the element with the id `control`, you would need to get the element that contains the file input control and then clear its value. 

Here is an example of how you can achieve this:

```javascript
$('#control').on('change', function() {
  var fileInput = $(this).closest('input[type="file"]');
  if (fileInput.length > 0) {
    fileInput.attr('value', '');
  }
});
```

This code attaches a change event listener to the file input control. When the file input control changes, it gets the closest input type "file" element (i.e., the file input control) and clears its value. 

However, if you are using a more complex setup with multiple input controls, this might not work as expected. In such cases, you would need to get the element that contains the file input control and then clear its value.

