Answers:
def invoke_function(func, ...args):
    return func(*args)

print(invoke_function(None, 10, 20))  # None, 10, 20
print(invoke_function(None, 10, 20, 'hello'))  # None, 10, 20, 'hello'
print(invoke_function(None, 10, 20, True))  # None, 10, 20, True
print(invoke_function(None, 10, 20, [1, 2, 3]))  # None, 10, 20, [1, 2, 3]

Questions:
Title: How do I make function decorators and chain them together?.
Body: <p>How do I make two decorators in Python that would do the following?</p>
<pre><code>@make_bold
@make_italic
def say():
   return &quot;Hello&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>Calling <code>say()</code> should return:</p>
<pre><code>&quot;&lt;b&gt;&lt;i&gt;Hello&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&quot;
</code></pre>

Answers:
```python
def make_bold(func):
    def wrapper():
        return "<b>" + func() + "</b>"
    return wrapper

def make_italic(func):
    def wrapper():
        return "<i>" + func() + "</i>"
    return wrapper

@make_bold
@make_italic
def say():
    return "Hello"

print(say())
```

In this code, `make_bold` and `make_italic` are two decorators that wrap the `say` function and apply the styles to it. The `@` symbol before the decorator function is used to indicate that it should be applied to the `say` function. The `say` function is called twice, once for each decorator, and the returned values are combined to form the final output.

```javascript
// Variables inside a function
function example() {
    let x = 10; // Local variable
    function outer() {
        let y = 20; // Local variable, outside the function
        console.log(x); // Accessing the local variable
        console.log(y);
    }
    outer();
}

// Variables defined globally
let x = 10; // Global variable
function outer() {
    let y = 20; // Local variable, outside the function
    console.log(x); // Accessing the global variable
    console.log(y);
}

example();
```

This will output:

```
10
20
10
20
```

In JavaScript, variables declared inside a function have the same scope as the function itself. This means that if a variable is declared inside a function, it will only be accessible within that function and any functions that call it. 

In contrast, variables declared outside a function have the same scope as the global scope. This means that they can be accessed from anywhere in the program, and can also be accessed from within a function.

Questions:
Title: Javascript call() &amp; apply() vs bind()?.
Body: <p>I already know that <code>apply</code> and <code>call</code> are similar functions which set <code>this</code> (context of a function).</p>
<p>The difference is with the way we send the arguments (manual vs array)</p>
<p><strong>Question:</strong></p>
<p>But when should I use the  <code>bind()</code> method ?</p>
<pre><code>var obj = {
  x: 81,
  getX: function() {
    return this.x;
  }
};

alert(obj.getX.bind(obj)());
alert(obj.getX.call(obj));
alert(obj.getX.apply(obj));
</code></pre>
<p><a href="http://jsbin.com/awewof/1/edit" rel="noreferrer">jsbin</a></p>

Answers:
```javascript
function obj() {
  this.x = 81;
}

// Using call()
var obj = new obj();
alert(obj.getX.call(obj));  // This is equivalent to obj.getX()
alert(obj.getX.call({}, obj));  // This is equivalent to obj.getX()

// Using apply()
var obj = new obj();
alert(obj.getX.apply(obj));  // This is equivalent to obj.getX()

// Using bind()
var obj = new obj();
alert(obj.getX.bind(obj));  // This is equivalent to obj.getX()
```

Questions:
Title: Is there a better way to do optional function parameters in JavaScript?.
Body: <p>I've always handled optional parameters in JavaScript like this:</p>

<pre><code>function myFunc(requiredArg, optionalArg){
  optionalArg = optionalArg || 'defaultValue';

  // Do stuff
}
</code></pre>

<p>Is there a better way to do it?</p>

<p>Are there any cases where using <code>||</code> like that is going to fail?</p>

Answers:
None
You can use the optional chaining operator (?.) in JavaScript to simplify optional parameter handling. The operator returns the second operand if the first operand is not null or undefined. Here's how you can use it:

```javascript
function myFunc(requiredArg, optionalArg) {
  const defaultValue = 'defaultValue';
  return optionalArg?.concat(defaultValue);
}
```

In this code, `optionalArg?.concat(defaultValue)` will return `'defaultValue'` if `optionalArg` is not null or undefined, and will return `undefined` if it is. This approach is more concise and expressive than the original `||` operator.

