Title: How to mock void methods with Mockito.
Body: <p>How to mock methods with void return type? </p>

<p>I implemented an observer pattern but I can't mock it with Mockito because I don't know how.</p>

<p>And I tried to find an example on the Internet but didn't succeed.</p>

<p>My class looks like this:</p>

<pre><code>public class World {

    List&lt;Listener&gt; listeners;

    void addListener(Listener item) {
        listeners.add(item);
    }

    void doAction(Action goal,Object obj) {
        setState("i received");
        goal.doAction(obj);
        setState("i finished");
    }

    private string state;
    //setter getter state
} 

public class WorldTest implements Listener {

    @Test public void word{
    World  w= mock(World.class);
    w.addListener(this);
    ...
    ...

    }
}

interface Listener {
    void doAction();
}
</code></pre>

<p>The system is not triggered with mock.</p>

<p>I want to show the above-mentioned system state. And make assertions according to them.</p>

Answers:
None
```python
import unittest
from unittest.mock import Mock
from typing import List, Callable, Any

class World:
    def __init__(self):
        self.listeners = []

    def addListener(self, item: Callable[[Any], None]) -> None:
        self.listeners.append(item)

    def doAction(self, goal: Any, obj: Any) -> None:
        print(f"State: received {obj}")
        goal.doAction(obj)
        print(f"State: finished")

class WorldTest(unittest.TestCase):
    def setUp(self):
        self.world = World()

    def test_mock_void_method(self):
        def mock_method(self):
            print("Mocked method called")

        mock_method.return_value = None  # Mocking void return type

        listener = self.world  # Assuming the listener is the same instance
        listener.addListener(mock_method)

        self.world.doAction(None, None)  # This should print "State: received None"
        self.world.doAction(None, None)  # This should print "State: finished"

if __name__ == "__main__":
    unittest.main()
```
In this code, we first define a `World` class with a `doAction` method that takes an `Action` object and an `Object` object as arguments. We then define a `WorldTest` class with a `setUp` method to create a new instance of `World`. In the `test_mock_void_method` method, we mock a `void` method by returning `None` from the `doAction` method. We then test the `doAction` method with a mock object and assert that it prints the expected messages.

