Questions:
Title: How do I create a Java string from the contents of a file?.
Body: <p>I've been using the idiom below for some time now. And it seems to be the most wide-spread, at least on the sites I've visited.</p>
<p>Is there a better/different way to read a file into a string in Java?</p>
<pre><code>private String readFile(String file) throws IOException {
    BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new FileReader (file));
    String line = null;
    StringBuilder stringBuilder = new StringBuilder();
    String ls = System.getProperty(&quot;line.separator&quot;);

    try {
        while((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
            stringBuilder.append(line);
            stringBuilder.append(ls);
        }

        return stringBuilder.toString();
    } finally {
        reader.close();
    }
}
</code></pre>

Answers:
```java
import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.FileReader;
import java.io.IOException;

public class Main {
    public static String readFile(String file) throws IOException {
        BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(file));
        StringBuilder stringBuilder = new StringBuilder();
        String lineSeparator = System.getProperty("line.separator");

        try {
            while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
                stringBuilder.append(line).append(lineSeparator);
            }
        } finally {
            reader.close();
        }

        return stringBuilder.toString();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String filePath = "path_to_your_file.txt"; // replace with your file path
        String content = readFile(filePath);
        System.out.println(content);
    }
}
```

This code reads the contents of a file into a string using the `readFile` method, which is a more concise and efficient way to achieve this task compared to the original idiom.

