```

In Scala, `class` is used to define a new class, while `object` is used to define a new object. The main difference between the two is that a class can have methods and fields, but an object can only have methods. This is because classes are designed to represent real-world entities, while objects are designed to represent instances of those entities.

class Vehicle {
    constructor(make, model, year) {
        this.make = make;
        this.model = model;
        this.year = year;
    }

    displayInfo() {
        console.log(`Make: ${this.make}, Model: ${this.model}, Year: ${this.year}`);
    }
}

class Car extends Vehicle {
    constructor(make, model, year, doors) {
        super(make, model, year);
        this.doors = doors;
    }

    displayInfo() {
        super.displayInfo();
        console.log(`Number of Doors: ${this.doors}`);
    }
}

class Truck extends Vehicle {
    constructor(make, model, year, capacity) {
        super(make, model, year);
        this.capacity = capacity;
    }

    displayInfo() {
        super.displayInfo();
        console.log(`Cargo Capacity: ${this.capacity}`);
    }
}

// Create instances of the classes
const car = new Car('Toyota', 'Camry', 2020, 4);
const truck = new Truck('Ford', 'F-150', 2019, 2.5);

// Call the displayInfo method on each instance
car.displayInfo();
truck.displayInfo();

